          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.501,
            0.069,
            0.816,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Introduction to Linear Polynomials"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.069,
            0.884,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "35"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.114,
            0.399,
            0.138
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.152,
            0.869,
            0.191
          ],
          "content": "Does this help you to conclude anything about the linear equation $y = a x + b$ when a is fixed but b varies?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.218,
            0.884,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** In these equations a = 2, and b takes the values –1, 1 and 5, respectively.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.257,
            0.886,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "Now let us draw the graphs of the equations $y = x + 3, \\\\ y = 2x + 5$ and $y = 3 x - 2$ . See Fig. 2.14 and observe where these lines cut the y-axis."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.312,
            0.729,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.459,
                0.661,
                0.541,
                0.68
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.14"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.693,
            0.289,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "We see that,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.72,
            0.532,
            0.74
          ],
          "content": "$y = 2x + 5$ cuts the y-axis at A (0, 5),"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.746,
            0.566,
            0.767
          ],
          "content": "$y = x + 3$ cuts the y-axis at B (0, 3), and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.773,
            0.54,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "$y = 3x - 2$ cuts the y-axis at C (0, –2)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.799,
            0.887,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "We observe that any straight line written in the form y = ax + b cuts the y-axis at the point (0, b). The length b is referred to as the y-intercept of the line. Thus, the y-intercept of the line y = x + 3 is 3. This means that the line cuts the y-axis at a distance of 3 units from the origin in the positive direction. Similarly, the y-intercept of the line $y = 3x - 2\\  is  -2$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
